 "fused_module": {
  "container": "TSRFUSE1",
  "fields": {
   "body": "shared",
   "columns": "shared",
   "family": "shared",
   "grid": "shared",
   "plane": "shared",
   "q256": "per_member",
   "rows": "per_member",
   "structure": "shared"
  },
  "fields_note": "Inside one vLLM-fused module (q/k/v, gate/up), what every role must SHARE and what is free PER MEMBER. vLLM builds one quant method per module, so the family -- and with it the grid, body, scale plane and input width the route decodes to one tile -- is a module fact. The RATE is not: every decoder in this build reads each role from that role's OWN manifest (fp8_route/bf16_route call prepare_window on unit.body_bits/unit.rates/unit.window_bits per role and concatenate; nvfp4_route packs each role's own rate/arity/memory/half scalars and decodes into that role's row slice). This block is checked against tessera.serving.scheme.FUSED_MODULE_FIELDS, the dict the loader itself gates on, exactly as tensor_parallel.units[].loader_axes is checked against sharding.ROUTE_TP_AXES.",
  "mixed_rung_receipt": false,
  "mixed_rung_receipt_note": "Whether a CONTAINER RECEIPT covers a served module whose members took different rungs. It is false: the relaxation is proven by a decode identity (a real Qwen3-0.6B q/k/v at three rungs decodes element-for-element to what the three roles decode alone -- experiments/fused_member_rung_identity.py, tests/test_fused_member_rungs.py), not by a vLLM serve. This is the same distinction loader_axes draws against max_world_size: what the loader accepts is not what a receipt has covered. lane_eligibility is UNCHANGED by this version, so a mixed-rung module's cells resolve exactly as its members' rungs do individually -- unattested where no cell names the rung, which is the honest status and not a refusal.",
  "schema": "tessera.fused-module.v1",
  "sidecar_q256": "int_or_per_role_list",
  "sidecar_q256_note": "How a fused module's rungs are spelled in the checkpoint's scheme. ONE field, one fact: config_groups[..].scheme.q256 is an integer when every role carries the same rung -- the spelling of every checkpoint written before contract v6, unchanged -- or a list of one integer per role, in the scheme's own roles order, when they differ. A plugin build older than v6 refuses the list form on sight ('q256 must be an integer'), which is the fail-closed direction; a v6 build reads both."
 },
